    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a city-capacity rule to the `POST /api/owners` endpoint that rejects creating an owner when the target city already contains 50 or more owners, responding with **409 Conflict**.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (modeled on the existing `RequireUniqueTelephone` duplicate-check step), I added four files and wired one step:\n\n1. **`RequireCityCapacity.java`** (`rest/function/owner/`) \u2014 a validation step that reads the published `OwnerFieldsDto`, counts existing owners in the requested city via `ownerRepository.findAll()`, and throws `CityAtCapacityException` when the count is \u2265 50. It runs before `build`, so the count reflects owners that already exist.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException.java`** (`rest/escalation/`) \u2014 a checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it appears in the `throws` clause.\n\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java`** (`rest/escalation/`) \u2014 responds `409 Conflict` with an RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail`, matching the existing `DuplicateTelephoneExceptionHandler` pattern.\n\n4. **`...CityAtCapacityException.yml`** (`officefloor/escalation/`) \u2014 maps the exception to its handler.\n\n5. Wired a new `capacity` step into `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` between `household` and `build`, governed by `transaction` like its neighbors.\n\n**Test results:** `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity()` (fills a city to 50 owners, expects the 51st POST to return 409) passes, and the full `./mvnw test` suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS, exit 0).",
